 "facts": [
  {
   "claim": "In the right (Hell) panel of Bosch's Garden of Earthly Delights, a choir sings from a score inscribed on a pair of buttocks; sinners are crucified by harp and lute ('Musicians' Hell')",
   "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Garden_of_Earthly_Delights ; https://earlymusicmuse.com/bosch1/"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Painted c. 1490–1500 (Prado); Wikipedia: between 1490 and 1510 — narrated as 'around 1500'",
   "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Garden_of_Earthly_Delights ; Museo del Prado collection page (copy: https://bpb-us-e1.wpmucdn.com/sites.psu.edu/dist/f/21472/files/2025/04/The-Garden-of-Earthly-Delights-Triptych-The-Collection-Museo-Nacional-del-Prado.html)"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Amelia Hamrick, a 20-year-old music student at Oklahoma Christian University; she and another student were looking at the painting one night; she transcribed it to modern notation (Feb 2014), recorded it and posted it on Tumblr",
   "source": "https://www.okgazette.com/?p=10507 ; https://www.medievalists.net/2014/02/500-year-old-song-found-butt-hell-sound-like/ ; https://earlymusicmuse.com/bosch1/"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Headlines: 'Student Transcribes 500-Year-Old \"Butt Song From Hell\"' (Music Feeds, 19 Feb 2014); artnet: 'Listen to Hieronymus Bosch's \"500-Year-Old Butt Song From Hell\"'",
   "source": "https://musicfeeds.com.au/news/student-transcribes-500-year-old-butt-song-from-hell/ ; https://news.artnet.com/in-brief/bosch-butt-song-from-hell-309732"
  },
  {
   "claim": "A university news release said she was the first to transcribe and the first to play the tune",
   "source": "https://www.okgazette.com/?p=10507"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Earlier transcriptions: J. Lenneberg, Gazette des Beaux-Arts, 1961, and Laurinda Dixon, 1981; Dixon: the 'discovery' had been common knowledge among art historians for over 50 years",
   "source": "https://earlymusicmuse.com/bosch1/"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Atrium Musicae (de Madrid), an early-music ensemble from Madrid founded by Gregorio Paniagua, recorded organum over the notes in 1978 (LP Codex Gluteo track)",
   "source": "https://earlymusicmuse.com/bosch1/ ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atrium_Musicae_de_Madrid"
  },
  {
   "claim": "The notation has no clef, so the pitch cannot be determined; Hamrick assumed the second line was C",
   "source": "https://earlymusicmuse.com/bosch1/ ; https://www.medievalists.net/2014/02/500-year-old-song-found-butt-hell-sound-like/"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Hamrick (AP): 'I think Bosch may have just been slapping notes up there to make it look like music.'",
   "source": "https://m.washingtontimes.com/news/2014/feb/23/okla-college-student-uncovers-hidden-song/ (AP, 23 Feb 2014) ; https://www.news9.com/story/24730944/okla-christian-university-student-solves-centuries-old-mystery"
  },
  {
   "claim": "The Garden of Earthly Delights hangs in the Museo del Prado, Madrid",
   "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Garden_of_Earthly_Delights"
  },
  {
   "claim": "(caption P.S.) The music book beneath the lute carries the same faux notation (strokes, notes placed randomly); Bosch also used it in The Haywain and The Last Judgement",
   "source": "https://earlymusicmuse.com/bosch1/"
  }
 ],
 "caption": "There's sheet music painted on a sinner's backside in Hieronymus Bosch's Hell, the right panel of The Garden of Earthly Delights (c. 1490–1500). Around him, sinners are crucified on a harp and a lute, and a choir sings from the notes on his skin.\n\nIn February 2014, Amelia Hamrick, a 20-year-old music student at Oklahoma Christian University, spotted it one night, wrote it out in modern notation, recorded it and posted it online. The headlines called it \"the 500-year-old butt song from Hell\", and her university said she was the first to play it.\n\nShe wasn't. Scholars had written the notes out by 1961, and in 1978 Atrium Musicae, an early-music group from Madrid, recorded them. And nobody can really play it: the staff has no clef, so the pitch is a guess. Even Hamrick told the AP: \"I think Bosch may have just been slapping notes up there to make it look like music.\"\n\nP.S. Bosch painted the same kind of make-believe notes in the open music book under the lute.\n\n🖤 Would you have noticed the notes?",
 "caption_short": "There's sheet music painted on this man's backside. Bosch's 'butt song from Hell' wasn't first played in 2014. 🖤",
 "youtube_title": "Who first played this? Bosch's Butt Song from Hell",
